From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tseng <tseng.jayj@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 10:30:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] wallet: make a fork scheduled at genesis active at genesis

wallet2::use_fork_rules(version, early_blocks) is the ONE predicate every
wallet-side rule selection goes through -- the fee algorithm, the dynamic fee,
2021 fee scaling, ring size, bulletproofs, bulletproof+, CLSAG and view tags.
Its arithmetic was inherited unchanged from Monero:

  height >= (int64_t)earliest_height - early_blocks

early_blocks is a TRANSITION OFFSET: positive starts the rules that many blocks
BEFORE the fork, negative waits that many blocks AFTER it. Monero's own callers
pass -10, -30 and -420 to stay clear of a live fork boundary, which is correct
on a chain that really passed through those forks.

MeepCoin does not replay Monero's fork history. Its schedule is ONE sparse
entry, v16 from height 0, so the daemon answers earliest_height = 0 for EVERY
known rule version. Subtracting a negative offset from zero then produces a
threshold ABOVE genesis for rules that are active IN the genesis block.

Measured on this chain at height 25:

  v8  per-byte fee      (offset   0)  -> active
  v4  dynamic fee       (offset -30)  -> INACTIVE  (threshold 30)
  v15 2021 fee scaling  (offset -30)  -> INACTIVE  (threshold 30)
  v3  fee algorithm 1   (offset -420) -> INACTIVE  (threshold 420)
  v15 bulletproof+      (offset -10)  -> INACTIVE  (threshold 10)

get_base_fee() therefore returned the pre-v8 legacy FEE_PER_KB, 2,000,000,000,
while create_transactions_2() was on the per-byte path and multiplied it by the
estimated weight. For the exact shape a 1 MEEP transfer takes here -- 1 input,
ring 16, 2 outputs, no extra, bulletproof+, CLSAG, view tags, 1,492 bytes --
that is a demanded fee of 2,984,000,000,000 atomic units (29.84 MEEP) against a
real network rate of 40,000/byte (59,680,000). The wallet refused its own valid
spend with "not enough money". No consensus rule, no fee constant, no decoy
selection and no actual shortage of funds was involved.

THE FIX IS IN THE PREDICATE, NOT IN A CALLER

src/wallet/fork_rules.h is new and holds the whole rule:

  - earliest_height == uint64 max (absent from the schedule) -> never active,
    exactly as before;
  - earliest_height == 0 -> active at every height, whatever offset the caller
    passed, because a fork scheduled at genesis has no transition to be early
    or late for;
  - any other fork height -> Monero's semantics EXACTLY, including the negative
    post-fork delays, now computed without wrap-around at either end (a caller
    passing INT64_MIN cannot be negated as int64_t, and earliest + delay cannot
    exceed uint64 max unnoticed).

use_fork_rules() calls it and does nothing else differently. No caller's
version, no caller's offset, no fee constant, no hard-fork schedule entry, no
decimal, no reward, no difficulty, no MeepHash input and no daemon-side fee
code is touched, and nothing is special-cased for testnet, for a height, for a
wallet or for a fee path. Every genesis-active negative-offset caller is fixed
by the same three lines.

WITNESSED BY A COMPILED REGRESSION

src/meepcoin_genesis/meepcoin_fork_activation_test.cpp (target
meepcoin-fork-activation-test) runs the production predicate, the production
hard-fork installer over the real compiled schedule, the production transaction
weight estimator and the production fee rounding. It asserts the genesis case
at heights 0/1/25/29/30 for offsets -420/-30/-10/0/+10/+30, the absent case,
the non-zero case boundary for boundary against the inherited expression over
heights 0..600, the int64 and uint64 extremes, that v1..v16 report earliest
height 0 while v17 does not exist, what each of wallet2's own call sites
selects, and the 1,492-byte / 40,000-per-byte / 59,680,000 arithmetic that the
refused transfer should have used. The inherited expression is kept in the test
as the witness of the defect: every place it appears, the test asserts it gives
the WRONG answer, so reverting this patch fails the regression.

wallet2::estimate_tx_weight_for_shape() is added for that test: a static
wrapper around the estimator create_transactions_2() already uses, so the
regression witnesses the production formula instead of copying it. It touches
no wallet state, no key and no daemon.

NOT ESTABLISHED BY THIS PATCH. It was compiled and its regression was run. No
wallet was opened, no transaction was constructed or signed, nothing was
relayed and no spend was attempted.
